---
/**
 * Search over the documentation, served from a static index and answered in
 * the reader's own browser.
 *
 * ---- why Pagefind, and what that buys ---------------------------------------
 *
 * The index is built by `pagefind` after `astro build`, from the HTML that
 * build just emitted, and shipped as files beside it. Querying it is a `fetch`
 * of a few index shards from this origin and some WebAssembly, and nothing
 * else. There is no service to call, no key to hold, and no query to log —
 * which is the point rather than a saving. This site's argument is that
 * `loading="interaction"` contacts no provider before a click, and a page that
 * posted every keystroke of its search box to a third party would be arguing
 * against itself. `e2e/site-search.spec.ts` asserts that no request during a
 * search leaves this origin, so the claim is observed rather than assumed.
 *
 * ---- what gets indexed ------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * Not decided here, and deliberately not a list of files. Pagefind walks the
 * built `dist/` and indexes every page that does not carry
 * `data-pagefind-ignore` on its `<body>`, which `Base.astro` writes for a page
 * that says it is not documentation. So the default is "indexed", and a
 * documentation page added later is searchable without anyone editing this
 * component, a glob, or a manifest. That default is the whole design: the
 * provider pages are being written in parallel and would otherwise land
 * unsearchable, with nothing failing to say so.
 *
 * The chrome is excluded separately and for a different reason: `data-
 * pagefind-ignore` sits on the site header and on a reference page's rail, so
 * a query does not match the package list that appears on every page. Without
 * that, every result's excerpt is the navigation.
 *
 * ---- the base path ----------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * Two URLs have to be right and both are derived from
 * `import.meta.env.BASE_URL` rather than written down: where the Pagefind
 * bundle is fetched from, and what a result's path is resolved against.
 * Pagefind records a result's URL as its path inside `dist/`, which is the
 * site root and is not the same string as the served prefix, so `baseUrl` is
 * what it has to be told. Getting either wrong fails silently — a search box
 * that finds nothing, or a result list of links that 404 — so
 * `e2e/site-search.spec.ts` runs the whole of this against a second build made
 * under a non-root prefix.
 *
 * ---- the keyboard -----------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * `/` opens and focuses, arrows move, Enter opens the highlighted result,
 * Escape dismisses. Only the first three of those are written here. A `<dialog>`
 * opened with `showModal()` brings Escape, the focus trap while it is open and
 * the return of focus to the button that opened it — three behaviours that are
 * not worth reimplementing and are easy to reimplement slightly wrong, and the
 * reason this is a `<dialog>` rather than a positioned `<div>`.
 *
 * The list is a combobox in the ARIA sense: focus stays in the input, and the
 * highlighted option is named by `aria-activedescendant`. That is what keeps
 * the results reachable by a screen reader while every keystroke still goes to
 * the field — moving real focus into the list on each arrow press would take
 * the caret out of the query the reader is still typing. Each option is a real
 * `<a>` underneath the role, so a result can also be middle-clicked or opened
 * in a new tab.
 *
 * With no JavaScript the button stays `hidden` and the dialog is never opened.
 * There is no static search to degrade to, so the honest degraded state is no
 * control at all rather than one that does nothing when pressed.
 */
---

<div class="search">
  {
    /* `hidden` in the markup and revealed by the script below. The script is
     * the whole of what this button does, so with no JavaScript there is nothing
     * to press rather than a control that swallows a click. Search has no static
     * form to degrade to — the index is queried by WebAssembly — so absence is
     * the honest degraded state. */
  }
  <button
    class="search__open"
    type="button"
    hidden
    aria-haspopup="dialog"
    data-search-open
  >
    <span>Search</span>
    <kbd class="search__key">/</kbd>
  </button>

  <dialog
    class="search__dialog"
    aria-label="Search the documentation"
    data-search-dialog
  >
    <label class="u-visually-hidden" for="docs-search-input">
      Search the documentation
    </label>
    {
      /* `type="text"` and not `type="search"`, which is a behaviour rather than
       * a preference: a search field consumes the first Escape to clear itself,
       * so a reader pressing Escape to dismiss the dialog watched their query
       * vanish and the dialog stay. Measured in Chromium, where the second
       * press then closes it. What `type="search"` buys is a clear affordance
       * for a pointer, and Escape dismissing the whole thing is the affordance
       * this dialog actually has. */
    }
    <input
      id="docs-search-input"
      class="search__input"
      type="text"
      placeholder="Search the documentation"
      autocomplete="off"
      spellcheck="false"
      role="combobox"
      aria-expanded="false"
      aria-controls="docs-search-results"
      aria-autocomplete="list"
      data-search-input
    />

    {
      /* The result count, announced. A listbox that changes under a reader who
       * cannot see it says nothing on its own, and this is the sentence that
       * makes "four results" audible. It is also the only place an empty query,
       * a failure to load the index and a genuine miss are told apart. */
    }
    <p class="search__status" role="status" data-search-status></p>

    <ul
      id="docs-search-results"
      class="search__results"
      role="listbox"
      aria-label="Search results"
      data-search-results
    >
    </ul>

    {
      /* One result, as markup rather than as `document.createElement` calls.
       * That is not tidiness: this component's `<style>` is scoped, which Astro
       * implements by stamping a `data-astro-cid-…` attribute onto the elements
       * it compiled and adding it to every selector. An element built by the
       * script carries no such attribute, so every rule below would match
       * nothing and the list would render as the browser's own underlined blue
       * links. Cloned from here, a row is one of this component's elements and
       * is styled like one.
       *
       * An `<a>` wearing `role="option"`, and out of the tab order. The role is
       * what a screen reader announces inside the listbox; the element
       * underneath it is what lets a result be middle-clicked or opened in a new
       * tab. Tab still leaves the field for the dialog's own stops rather than
       * walking eight results. */
    }
    <template data-search-template>
      <a class="search__result" role="option" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">
        <span class="search__result-title"></span>
        <span class="search__result-excerpt"></span>
      </a>
    </template>
  </dialog>
</div>

<script>
  /*
   * `import.meta.env.BASE_URL` is substituted by the build, so both of these
   * are constants in the shipped file rather than something the page works out
   * at runtime. Written as a literal `/pagefind/` they would be identical
   * today, which is exactly why they are not: the prefix is the one thing here
   * that is wrong silently.
   */
  const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
  const bundle = `${base}pagefind/pagefind.js`;

  /** How many results the list shows. */
  const LIMIT = 8;

  /*
   * Pagefind's own debounce. It resolves to `null` for a search that a later
   * keystroke has superseded, which is what keeps a slow query from painting
   * over a fast one typed after it.
   */
  const DEBOUNCE_MS = 180;

  const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.search');
  const openButton = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>(
    '[data-search-open]'
  );
  const dialog = document.querySelector<HTMLDialogElement>(
    '[data-search-dialog]'
  );
  const input = document.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-search-input]');
  const status = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-search-status]');
  const list = document.querySelector<HTMLUListElement>(
    '[data-search-results]'
  );
  const template = document.querySelector<HTMLTemplateElement>(
    '[data-search-template]'
  );

  /**
   * One flattened entry in the list: a heading inside a document, or a document
   * with no headings to offer.
   */
  interface Entry {
    title: string;
    url: string;
    excerpt: string;
  }

  /*
   * The Pagefind bundle is generated at build time and has no types to import.
   * Only the three members this file calls are described, so a change in the
   * shape of a result is a type error here rather than an empty list at
   * runtime.
   */
  interface PagefindResultData {
    url: string;
    excerpt: string;
    meta: { title?: string };
    sub_results?: { title: string; url: string; excerpt: string }[];
  }

  interface Pagefind {
    options: (options: { baseUrl: string }) => Promise<void>;
    init: () => Promise<void>;
    debouncedSearch: (
      query: string,
      options: Record<string, never>,
      debounce: number
    ) => Promise<{ results: { data: () => Promise<PagefindResultData> }[] } | null>;
  }

  if (root && openButton && dialog && input && status && list && template) {
    /*
     * Loaded on the first query and not before. Measured on this site in
     * chromium, a first query pulls 221 KB over eight requests — the module, a
     * worker, the entry, the metadata, the WebAssembly, an index chunk and a
     * fragment per matching document — and a reader who never searches should
     * pay for none of it. Holding the promise here is what makes the second
     * query reuse the first one's load rather than starting another.
     */
    let loading: Promise<Pagefind> | undefined;

    const load = () => {
      loading ??= (async () => {
        /* The specifier is a runtime string because the prefix is not known
         * until the build substitutes it, and the bundle does not exist until
         * after the build has run — so there is nothing for the bundler to
         * resolve at this point even in principle. */
        const pagefind = (await import(/* @vite-ignore */ bundle)) as Pagefind;
        /* What a result's path is resolved against. Pagefind records the path
         * a page has inside `dist/`, which is the site root and not the prefix
         * the site is served under. */
        await pagefind.options({ baseUrl: base });
        await pagefind.init();
        return pagefind;
      })();
      return loading;
    };

    /** The entries currently rendered, in the order they appear. */
    let entries: Entry[] = [];
    /** Which of them is highlighted, or -1 when the list is empty. */
    let active = -1;

    const optionId = (index: number) => `docs-search-result-${index}`;

    const highlight = (next: number) => {
      const previous = list.children[active];
      if (previous instanceof HTMLElement) {
        previous.setAttribute('aria-selected', 'false');
      }
      active = next;
      const current = list.children[active];
      if (current instanceof HTMLElement) {
        current.setAttribute('aria-selected', 'true');
        input.setAttribute('aria-activedescendant', current.id);
        /* `nearest` rather than `center`: the list scrolls only when the
         * highlighted row is off the end of it, so travelling through a short
         * list does not move the rows under the reader's eye. */
        current.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest' });
      } else {
        input.removeAttribute('aria-activedescendant');
      }
    };

    const render = (query: string, found: Entry[]) => {
      entries = found;
      list.replaceChildren(
        ...found.map((entry, index) => {
          const row = template.content.cloneNode(true) as DocumentFragment;
          const option = row.querySelector('a');
          const title = row.querySelector('.search__result-title');
          const excerpt = row.querySelector('.search__result-excerpt');
          if (option === null || title === null || excerpt === null) {
            throw new Error('The result template has lost one of its parts.');
          }

          option.id = optionId(index);
          option.href = entry.url;
          title.textContent = entry.title;
          /* Markup, because Pagefind wraps the matched words in `<mark>` and
           * that is the whole value of an excerpt. What it wraps is this site's
           * own built pages, escaped by Pagefind on the way in — there is no
           * path by which a reader's query becomes markup here. */
          excerpt.innerHTML = entry.excerpt;

          return option;
        })
      );

      input.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(found.length > 0));
      highlight(found.length > 0 ? 0 : -1);

      if (query === '') {
        status.textContent = '';
      } else if (found.length === 0) {
        status.textContent = `No results for “${query}”.`;
      } else {
        status.textContent = `${found.length} result${found.length === 1 ? '' : 's'} for “${query}”.`;
      }
    };

    /*
     * A document is one result and its headings are its sub-results, and it is
     * the headings that are worth showing. A reference page is an entire
     * README, so "@playdeck/core matched" is barely an answer — the reader
     * wants the section, and the sub-result carries the fragment that lands
     * them in it. A result with no sub-results at all falls back to the page:
     * one entry that goes somewhere is worth more than a document that
     * disappears from a list it matched.
     */
    const flatten = (data: PagefindResultData): Entry[] => {
      const subs = data.sub_results ?? [];
      if (subs.length === 0) {
        return [
          {
            title: data.meta.title ?? data.url,
            url: data.url,
            excerpt: data.excerpt
          }
        ];
      }
      return subs.map((sub) => ({
        title: sub.title,
        url: sub.url,
        excerpt: sub.excerpt
      }));
    };

    const run = async (query: string) => {
      if (query === '') {
        render('', []);
        return;
      }

      let pagefind: Pagefind;
      try {
        pagefind = await load();
      } catch {
        /* The bundle would not load or would not start. Whatever the cause —
         * a deployment that shipped the pages without the index, a request that
         * failed — saying so is better than an empty list, which a reader would
         * read as "nothing matched". */
        render(query, []);
        status.textContent = 'Search is unavailable on this page.';
        return;
      }

      const search = await pagefind.debouncedSearch(query, {}, DEBOUNCE_MS);
      /* Superseded by a later keystroke while this one was in flight. */
      if (search === null) return;
      /* And superseded by one that arrived while the data was being read. */
      if (input.value.trim() !== query) return;

      const pages = await Promise.all(
        search.results.slice(0, LIMIT).map((result) => result.data())
      );
      render(query, pages.flatMap(flatten).slice(0, LIMIT));
    };

    input.addEventListener('input', () => {
      void run(input.value.trim());
    });

    input.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
      if (entries.length === 0) return;

      if (event.key === 'ArrowDown') {
        event.preventDefault();
        highlight((active + 1) % entries.length);
      } else if (event.key === 'ArrowUp') {
        event.preventDefault();
        highlight((active - 1 + entries.length) % entries.length);
      } else if (event.key === 'Enter') {
        const entry = entries[active];
        if (entry !== undefined) {
          /* The field is inside a `<form>`-less dialog, so nothing would submit
           * — but Enter with a result highlighted means "open that one", and
           * the default would be to do nothing at all. */
          event.preventDefault();
          window.location.href = entry.url;
        }
      }
    });

    openButton.hidden = false;
    openButton.addEventListener('click', () => {
      dialog.showModal();
      input.focus();
      input.select();
    });

    /*
     * The shortcut. Ignored while the reader is typing somewhere else, which on
     * this site is only ever this same field, and ignored when a modifier is
     * held so that browser and operating-system bindings keep working.
     */
    document.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
      if (event.key !== '/' || dialog.open) return;
      if (event.metaKey || event.ctrlKey || event.altKey) return;
      const target = event.target;
      if (
        target instanceof HTMLInputElement ||
        target instanceof HTMLTextAreaElement ||
        (target instanceof HTMLElement && target.isContentEditable)
      ) {
        return;
      }
      /* Otherwise the character the shortcut is spelt with lands in the field
       * the shortcut just focused. */
      event.preventDefault();
      dialog.showModal();
      input.focus();
      input.select();
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  .search {
    display: flex;
  }

  /* A button rather than a field in the bar. At 320px the header already holds
   * a truncating breadcrumb and the theme switch, and a third flexible box
   * between them leaves a field too narrow to read a query in. The button also
   * carries the shortcut, which is the thing a reader has to be told once. */
  .search__open {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    align-items: center;
    min-block-size: var(--hit-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-3);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    cursor: pointer;
    background-color: var(--color-sunken);
    border: var(--line-width) solid var(--color-line-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

  .search__open:hover {
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* Pressed, the fourth state every control on this site carries. */
  .search__open:active {
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  /* The shortcut, at the functional floor. At 40rem and up it is rendered and
   * not hidden from assistive technology: the shortcut is real, and a reader who
   * cannot see the key cap still wants to know it exists.
   *
   * Below 40rem it is not rendered at all, and `display: none` rather than a
   * visual trick because there it should leave the accessible name too — the
   * button reads "Search" on a phone, which is all it can do there. The header
   * at 360px is a wordmark, a truncating trail, this button and the switch, and
   * a key cap is the one thing in that row addressed to a device the reader
   * probably does not have. The width it costs comes straight out of the
   * breadcrumb: measured at 360px in chromium, the `Reference` crumb shows 18px
   * of its 79 with the cap rendered and 43px without it. */
  .search__key {
    display: none;
    padding-inline: var(--space-1);
    font-size: var(--text-fn);
    color: var(--color-ink-subtle);
    border: var(--line-width) solid var(--color-line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  }

  @media (min-width: 40rem) {
    .search__key {
      display: revert;
    }
  }

  /* An elevated surface, so it carries no border — see `DESIGN.md` rule 4,
   * which names this dialog as one of the three things allowed to spend an
   * elevation. `--elevation-panel` and not the instrument: the instrument is
   * the panel a page is built around, and this one is a panel over a page. */
  .search__dialog {
    inline-size: min(38rem, calc(100vw - var(--space-6)));
    max-block-size: min(32rem, calc(100vh - var(--space-8)));
    padding: var(--space-4);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-8);
    margin-inline: auto;
    overflow: hidden;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    border: none;
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    box-shadow: var(--elevation-panel);
  }

  /* The document behind the dialog, dimmed. A token at reduced opacity rather
   * than a colour of its own, so the scrim is the page's own field in both
   * themes and the system gains no fourth grey. */
  .search__dialog::backdrop {
    background-color: var(--color-field);
    opacity: 0.75;
  }

  /* A recessed well, which is what `--color-sunken` is for, with the control
   * boundary that non-text UI owes 3:1. */
  .search__input {
    inline-size: 100%;
    min-block-size: var(--hit-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-3);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    background-color: var(--color-sunken);
    border: var(--line-width) solid var(--color-line-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

  .search__input::placeholder {
    color: var(--color-ink-subtle);
  }

  .search__status {
    padding-block-start: var(--space-2);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-fn);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-fn);
    color: var(--color-ink-subtle);
  }

  /* The list scrolls inside the dialog rather than growing it, so the field
   * stays where it was put. `thin` for the same reason the rail and a code
   * block take it: a platform-width bar inside a panel this small reads as a
   * second border. */
  .search__results {
    max-block-size: 20rem;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    overflow-y: auto;
    list-style: none;
    scrollbar-width: thin;
  }

  .search__result {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-1);
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

  /* The highlighted row. Focus never leaves the field — the row is named by
   * `aria-activedescendant` instead — so this is the only thing on screen
   * saying which result Enter will open, and it is a filled row rather than the
   * site's focus ring so that the two are not mistaken for each other. */
  .search__result[aria-selected='true'] {
    background-color: var(--color-sunken);
  }

  .search__result-title {
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  /* Held to three lines. Pagefind's excerpt is as long as the passage the match
   * sits in, and these documents are mostly code, so an unclamped row ran to a
   * dozen lines while the one under it ran to two — a list nobody can travel
   * with arrow keys, because the next row is never where the eye expects it.
   * Three lines is enough to see the match in its sentence. `line-clamp` and the
   * `-webkit-` spelling together, because the unprefixed property is the newer
   * of the two and the older one is what most engines still ship. */
  .search__result-excerpt {
    display: -webkit-box;
    overflow: hidden;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
    -webkit-line-clamp: 3;
    line-clamp: 3;
  }

  /* Pagefind wraps the matched words in `<mark>`, whose default is a yellow
   * belonging to no design system on a background this one never chose. Weight
   * and the accent instead: two channels, so the match is not colour alone. */
  .search__result-excerpt :global(mark) {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    background-color: transparent;
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }
</style>
